<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tughtakin ibn Ayyub</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1197</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sayf al-Islam Tughtakin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tughtakin ibn Ayyub, titled Sayf al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, was a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> who ruled Ayyubid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from 1182 until his death in 1197. Sent to restore order after the conquest by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a>, he took Sanaa and other highland strongholds from local rulers and built up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Ta'izz</a> as a base. His rule tied Yemen's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> trade, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>'s customs revenue, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>.</p></div>
